========================================================================================================================
k03 -- THE HI SIZE-MASS RELATION: is the tightest relation in extragalactic astronomy an a_0 law?
========================================================================================================================
  Sigma_M = a_0/(2 pi G) = 106.9 Msun/pc^2  (canonical)
  Sigma_M = a_0/(2 pi G) = 129.0 Msun/pc^2  (alt)

  109 SPARC discs have a tabulated HI radius that their rotation curve reaches (38 need <=15% extrapolation).

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 0 -- reproduce the published relation on this sample, or the rest of the item means nothing
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  log D_HI = 0.509 log M_HI + -3.302,  scatter 0.070 dex, N = 109
  published (Wang+2016, 562 galaxies): slope 0.506, intercept -3.293, scatter 0.06 dex
  [PASS] K03-0 the SPARC sample must reproduce the published HI size-mass relation, or it is not the relation under test   (slope 0.509 vs 0.506, scatter 0.070 dex vs 0.06 (SPARC's R_HI is a catalogue value with its own distance errors, so a somewhat larger scatter is expected))

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
K03-A  R_HI = xi sqrt(G M_HI/a_0).   Is xi one number, and is it predicted?
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  canonical  median xi =   7.54   scatter 0.070 dex   d log xi/d log M_HI = +0.009
  alt        median xi =   8.29   scatter 0.070 dex   d log xi/d log M_HI = +0.009
  [PASS] K03-A xi must be one number with RAR-class scatter AND no mass trend.  It can fail on either.   (median xi = 7.54, scatter 0.070 dex, mass slope +0.009)

  AND THE DECISIVE POINT, which no amount of tightness can rescue: is xi = 7.54 PREDICTED?
  The framework fixes a_0 and therefore r_M.  It says nothing whatever about where hydrogen stops being
  neutral.  xi is a number about self-shielding and the ionising background, and the framework has no
  handle on it.  So even a perfect K03-A would fail criterion (2) of a Kepler-grade law: the coefficient
  is fitted, not predicted.  Recorded as such.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
K03-B/C  is the ACCELERATION at the HI edge universal?  (this is the form in which a_0 could appear)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  footing   quantity      median /a_0  scatter [dex]   d log/d log M_b  d log/d log g_bar
  canonical g_bar(R_HI)        0.0708          0.390             0.313                   
  canonical g_obs(R_HI)        0.3247          0.274             0.202                   
  alt       g_bar(R_HI)        0.0587          0.390             0.313                   
  alt       g_obs(R_HI)        0.2689          0.274             0.202                   
  [FAIL] K03-B g_bar at the HI edge must be a universal fraction of a_0 with <= 0.1 dex scatter and no mass trend   (median 0.0708 a_0, scatter 0.390 dex, mass slope +0.313)
  [FAIL] K03-C g_obs at the HI edge must be a universal fraction of a_0 with <= 0.1 dex scatter and no mass trend   (median 0.3247 a_0, scatter 0.274 dex, mass slope +0.202)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
K03-D  the HI edge as a surface density.  Sigma_HI(<R_HI) = M_HI/(pi R_HI^2), against Sigma_M = a_0/(2 pi G)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  mean HI surface density inside R_HI: median 3.76 Msun/pc^2, scatter 0.140 dex, mass slope -0.018
  Sigma_M/Sigma_HI = 28.4   (canonical) -- is that a number the framework predicts?  No.
  Sigma_M/Sigma_HI = 34.3   (alt) -- is that a number the framework predicts?  No.
  [FAIL] K03-D the HI edge's mean surface density must be a PREDICTED fraction of Sigma_M = a_0/(2 pi G).  The framework predicts no such fraction, so this check is written to fail unless the ratio lands on a simple number (1, 2, pi, 2 pi, 4 pi) to better than 5%   (ratio 28.45 (canonical), 34.34 (alt) -- no simple number within 5%)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE UPSILON LEVER, numerically
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  d log g_bar(R_HI) / d log Upsilon = +0.441
  d log g_obs(R_HI) / d log Upsilon = +0.000  (zero by construction: g_obs has no stellar mass in it)
  d log xi / d log Upsilon         = 0.000 exactly (xi uses M_HI, not M_star)
  So K03-A and K03-C are among the very few statements in this hunt with NO stellar mass-to-light leverage
  at all.  That is the good news.  The bad news is criterion (2): none of their coefficients is predicted.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
MUTATION CONTROLS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  [PASS] M03a shuffling which galaxy's HI mass goes with which galaxy's HI radius must destroy the tightness -- if it does not, the relation is an artefact of the sample's dynamic range and not a relation at all   (shuffled scatter 0.917 dex against the true 0.140 dex)
  [PASS] M03b quadrupling a_0 must move xi by exactly a factor 2, since xi carries a_0^(1/2) -- and note what that means: xi is an a_0 METER with leverage 1/2, so its 0.1 dex scatter caps any a_0 read from it at 0.2 dex   (shift +0.3010 dex against the predicted +0.301)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE NEWTONIAN / LambdaCDM ALTERNATIVE, COMPUTED BESIDE
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The standard explanation of the HI size-mass relation needs no gravity theory at all: hydrogen goes
  neutral below a column set by the ionising background and self-shielding, so the mean surface density
  inside the 1 Msun/pc^2 isophote is fixed, and M_HI = pi R_HI^2 <Sigma> follows with slope exactly 1/2.
  Measured here: <Sigma> = 3.76 Msun/pc^2 with 0.140 dex of scatter and a
  mass slope of -0.018.  That is the whole relation, and there is no acceleration in it.
  Both theories therefore predict slope 1/2 for the same reason: neither predicts it.  The MOND radius also
  goes as M^(1/2), which is why the coincidence looked like something.

========================================================================================================================
THE RESTATEMENT TEST
========================================================================================================================
  K03-A: R_HI = xi sqrt(G M_HI/a_0).  Derive from v^4 = G M_b a_0?  v_flat^2 = sqrt(G M_b a_0) = G M_b/r_M, so
  r_M = G M_b/v_flat^2 -- pure algebra from the BTFR.  So r_M is a BTFR quantity, and K03-A is the statement
  'R_HI is proportional to r_M', i.e. a restatement PLUS an unexplained proportionality constant.  CLOSES.
  K03-B/C: g at a radius fixed by an atomic-physics threshold.  Not derivable from the BTFR (the BTFR has no
  radius), but also not predicted by the framework, because nothing in the framework fixes where hydrogen
  ionises.  DOES NOT CLOSE as a restatement, but fails criterion (2) instead: no predicted coefficient.
  K03-D: definitional.  CLOSES trivially.

========================================================================================================================
VERDICT -- k03: NO.  The tightest scaling relation in extragalactic astronomy is not an a_0 law.
========================================================================================================================
  * R_HI does track the MOND radius of its own HI mass, with median xi = 7.54 (canonical) / 8.29 (alt),
    scatter 0.070 dex and a mass slope of +0.009.  But xi is not predicted by anything, and
    the tracking is forced: both quantities go as M_HI^(1/2), one because surface density is fixed by
    atomic physics and the other because a_0 is fixed by Lambda.  Two square roots meeting is not a law.
  * The acceleration at the HI edge is NOT universal: g_bar spans 0.390 dex with a mass slope of +0.313,
    g_obs spans 0.274 dex with a mass slope of +0.202.  Both fail the 0.1 dex bar and both
    carry a mass trend, so there is no acceleration scale hiding at the edge of the HI disc.
  * Reported against interest, and it removes a candidate the programme might otherwise have chased: the
    coincidence between the HI size-mass slope and the MOND-radius slope is exactly that.
  * One thing worth keeping for elsewhere: g_obs(R_HI) and xi have EXACTLY ZERO stellar mass-to-light
    leverage.  If a quantity of that kind can be found whose coefficient IS predicted, it would be the
    cleanest measurement in the hunt.  This one is not it.

